  "proof": "We claim that S(n) = (n+1)! - 1 for all n >= 1.\n\nProof by induction:\nBase case: For n = 1, S(1) = 1*1! = 1. The formula gives (1+1)! - 1 = 2! - 1 = 2 - 1 = 1. The base case holds.\n\nInductive step: Assume that for some integer k >= 1, S(k) = (k+1)! - 1. We want to show that S(k+1) = (k+2)! - 1.\nBy definition, S(k+1) = S(k) + (k+1)*(k+1)!.\nSubstituting the inductive hypothesis:\nS(k+1) = [(k+1)! - 1] + (k+1)*(k+1)!\n= (k+1)! * [1 + (k+1)] - 1\n= (k+1)! * (k+2) - 1\n= (k+2)! - 1.\n\nThus, by the principle of mathematical induction, S(n) = (n+1)! - 1 for all n >= 1."
